    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Can you finish that breakdown for the low-GI foods, and also maybe provide some practical tips on how a patient can balance these in a single meal? Like, how do they mix high and low GI items without causing a massive spike?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Low-Glycemic Foods (The \"Steady\" Sources)\nThese include whole grains, legumes, most non-starchy vegetables, and berries. They act like a slow-release battery, feeding the body glucose at a manageable pace. For a Type 2 diabetic, this allows the pancreas to produce just enough insulin to handle the load without getting overwhelmed.\n\n**Practical Tips for Balancing a Meal**\nTo prevent a massive spike, patients should aim to pair high-GI foods with low-GI foods and add fiber or protein. Think of it like mixing a volatile chemical (high-GI) with a stabilizing agent (low-GI) in a lab; the stabilizer slows down the reaction.\n\n1.  **The \"Sandwich\" Method:** If you must eat a high-GI item like white rice or a bagel, wrap it in a low-GI base like a large salad or a side of roasted broccoli. The fiber and volume of the low-GI food physically slow down the digestion of the high-GI item.\n2.  **Protein as a Brake:** Adding a source of protein (like chicken, fish, or tofu) acts like a brake pedal on the",
      "source": "target",
